    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R1 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R1">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R1_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeEnergy"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Energy R1 - Own energy field</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every radioactive molecule in use has an energy field.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: does a radioactive molecule have its radiation field? Applies to: every Molecule in use whose library entry says it is radioactive (cpo:isRadioactive = true, read through hlo:specializationOf); all other molecules are not affected, since most species create no field of their own. Not to Fixed molecules: a library entry describes a species in general and need not carry a field; the molecule in use, which reads the flag through the chain, must have one (rule 7.7 of the crate rules). Checks: the molecule has at least one field of its own (cpo:hasEnergyField) - for a molecule, a field it creates itself is a radiation field. Its fields on the higher levels are collected along the chain of the field (Info). Reports: the flag is set and the molecule has no field. Not here: the fields the molecule is exposed to - of its phase, material, device, room and site - are collected by Molecule_Energy_R2. Same pattern as Device_Energy_R5 and Phase_Energy_R4. An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R1_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R1_MainShape"/>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R2 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R2">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#SurroundingsRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R2_ContextPathShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeEnergy"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Energy R2 - Energy fields around a molecule</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Collects every energy field a molecule is exposed to: its own, its phase's, its material's, its device's, its room's and its site's. Nothing is required.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: which energy fields act on this molecule? Applies to: every Molecule. Collects: every EnergyField it is exposed to. The path first climbs to whatever holds it - the Phase it is part of, the MaterialRepresentation of that phase, the Device containing that material (for a stream that no device contains, the devices at its ends: cpo:flowsFrom, cpo:flowsTo, then cpo:isPortOf), the ambient air of the same level, the Room and the Site of that room - and then goes down from each of those into the rest of the set-up, also into every device standing in the room and into what stands inside it (cpo:isLocationOf, set by the generator within the crate and on the same level only): every field in a room acts everywhere in it, however weak - how strongly is the value of the field, not a matter of structure. Each field comes with what is recorded on its higher levels (hlo:specializationOf). The same path as Device_Energy_R6 and Phase_Energy_R5. Not here: the molecule's own field is required by Molecule_Energy_R1. A surroundings requirement (rule 7.16 of the crate rules): Info only and no minimum count, and what it collects is not linked (rule 6.3). A molecule need not sit in any field.</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R2_ContextPathShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R2_ContextPathShape"/>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R3 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R3">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#SurroundingsRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R3_ContextPathShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeEnergy"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Energy R3 - Temperature of its phase</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Collects the temperature of the phase a molecule is part of. Nothing is required.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: at which temperature is this molecule? Applies to: every Molecule. Collects: the temperature of the Phase it is part of (dcterms:isPartOf), on every level along the chain of the value (hlo:specializationOf). A molecule has no temperature of its own; it experiences that of its phase. Not here: the phase itself requires its temperature (Phase_Energy_R1); the temperatures of what holds and touches the phase are collected by Phase_Energy_R6 and R8. A surroundings requirement (rule 7.16 of the crate rules): Info only and no minimum count, and what it collects is not linked (rule 6.3).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R3_ContextPathShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R3_ContextPathShape"/>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R4 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R4">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#SurroundingsRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R4_ContextPathShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeEnergy"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Energy R4 - Pressure of its phase</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Collects the pressure of the phase a molecule is part of. Nothing is required.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: at which pressure is this molecule? Applies to: every Molecule. Collects: the pressure of the Phase it is part of (dcterms:isPartOf), on every level along the chain of the value (hlo:specializationOf). A molecule has no pressure of its own; it experiences that of its phase. Not here: the phase itself requires its pressure (Phase_Energy_R2); the pressures of what holds and touches the phase are collected by Phase_Energy_R7 and R9. A surroundings requirement (rule 7.16 of the crate rules): Info only and no minimum count, and what it collects is not linked (rule 6.3).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R4_ContextPathShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Energy_R4_ContextPathShape"/>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R2 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R2">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R2_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeInformation"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Information R2 - CAS number</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every molecule has a CAS number, unless its library entry states that none exists.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: under which CAS number is this substance registered? Applies to: every Molecule, library entries included: the value stands once, at the Fixed entry in the chemical entity library, and every level reads it from there through hlo:specializationOf. Checks: the molecule reaches a CAS number (cpo:hasCAS), or its library entry states that none exists (cpo:hasNoCASNumber = true). For radicals, reaction intermediates and newly made compounds there is none; saying so in the library tells "there is none" from "nobody looked it up". Reports: neither is found. Not here: the format of the number is checked in the data quality shapes (hlo:ChemicalEntity_Format_Shape, rule 2.8 of the crate rules), its check digit by a consistency check of the libraries; the PubChem CID is Molecule_Information_R3. An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R2_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R2_MainShape"/>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R4 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R4">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R4_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeInformation"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Information R4 - InChIKey</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every molecule has an InChIKey.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: under which key do databases find this molecule? Applies to: every Molecule, library entries included: the value stands once, at the Fixed entry in the chemical entity library, and every level reads it from there through hlo:specializationOf. Checks: the molecule reaches an InChIKey (cpo:hasInChIKey), the fixed-length hash of the InChI that databases use to look a molecule up. Reports: no InChIKey is found - also for a molecule that no database lists yet, such as an idealized solid made in the experiment: an identifier is not guessed, and the report is accepted (a known limitation). Not here: the InChI itself, which describes the structure (Molecule_Structure_Substance_R1); the standard format of the key (27 characters) is checked in the data quality shapes (hlo:ChemicalEntity_Format_Shape, rule 2.8 of the crate rules). An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R4_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R4_MainShape"/>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R3 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R3">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R3_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeInformation"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Information R3 - PubChem CID</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every molecule has a PubChem CID, unless its library entry states that PubChem has none.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: under which compound ID does PubChem list this substance? Applies to: every Molecule, library entries included: the value stands once, at the Fixed entry in the chemical entity library, and every level reads it from there through hlo:specializationOf. Checks: the molecule reaches a PubChem Compound ID (cpo:hasPubChemCID), or its library entry states that PubChem has no record for it (cpo:hasNoPubChemCID = true) - a reaction intermediate or a newly made compound; the explicit statement tells "there is none" from "nobody looked it up". Reports: neither is found. Not here: that the CID is a positive integer is checked in the data quality shapes (hlo:ChemicalEntity_Format_Shape, rule 2.8 of the crate rules); the CAS number is Molecule_Information_R2. An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R3_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R3_MainShape"/>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R1 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R1">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R1_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeInformation"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Information R1 - Library link</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every molecule in use is linked to its entry in the chemical entity library.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: which substance is this molecule? Applies to: every Molecule in use. Not to Fixed molecules: they are library entries themselves, the end of the chain (rule 7.7 of the crate rules). Checks: up the chain (hlo:specializationOf, one or more steps) it reaches a Fixed molecule - its entry in the chemical entity library; a molecule of a phase that comes from a library (the content of a container or of a supply line, the room air) passes the record in the library entry of that phase on the way. Through this chain a molecule in use reads what stands once at the entry - InChI, CAS number, SMILES, formula, molar mass - without repeating it. The Fixed molecules reached are linked: the entry, and the record on the way where there is one. Reports: no Fixed molecule is reached. Not here: the identifiers themselves (Molecule_Information_R2, R3, R4, Molecule_Structure_Substance_R1, Molecule_Structure_R2, Molecule_Substance_R2), the molar mass (Molecule_Substance_R3). Like Device_Information_R1. An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R1_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R1_MainShape"/>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R2 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R2">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#AssignmentRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R2_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeTime"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Time R2 - Opening of the containers upstream</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Assigns to the time context of a molecule the opening time of every container its phase's material may have come from. Nothing is required.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: when were the containers opened that this molecule can come from? Applies to: every Molecule. The counterpart of Phase_Time_R5 for the molecule: the same path and the same value shape, with one step in front - from the molecule up to its phase (dcterms:isPartOf). Assigns: the opening time (cpo:hasOpeningTime at the container, read through hlo:specializationOf) of the container its phase's material is in and of every container on the way back - backwards over the transfers and up the chain, any number of steps in any order. The time is linked. Known limit, to be addressed later: the assignment works per phase, not per substance. A molecule is assigned every candidate of its phase, also contents that never held its substance - ammonia formed in a reactor is assigned the bottles of the reactants. Following only the transfers that move its substance (cpo:transfersChemicalEntity) and only the contents that hold it would need SHACL-SPARQL (a known limitation). Not here: the arrivals (Molecule_Time_R1), expiry or re-test (Molecule_Time_R3), the containers themselves (Molecule_Time_R4); that the material of the phase has a known origin is checked at the phase - the arrival time of a container content by Phase_Time_R1, an origin for every other phase by Phase_Time_R2 - and not again at every molecule, so a gap is reported once. An assignment (rule 7.16 of the crate rules): nothing is required, the shape never reports.</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R2_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R2_MainShape"/>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R3 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R3">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#AssignmentRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R3_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeTime"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Time R3 - Expiry or re-test of the materials upstream</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Assigns to the time context of a molecule the expiry or re-test time of every material its phase's material may have come from. Nothing is required.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: until when may the materials be used that this molecule can come from? Applies to: every Molecule. The counterpart of Phase_Time_R6 for the molecule: the same path and the same value shape, with one step in front - from the molecule up to its phase (dcterms:isPartOf). Assigns: the expiry or re-test time (cpo:hasExpiryTime, cpo:hasRetestTime) of the material of its phase and of every material on the way back. A stable substance has a re-test date instead of an expiry date (rule 7.17 of the crate rules). The time is linked. Known limit, to be addressed later: the assignment works per phase, not per substance. A molecule is assigned every candidate of its phase, also contents that never held its substance - ammonia formed in a reactor is assigned the bottles of the reactants. Following only the transfers that move its substance (cpo:transfersChemicalEntity) and only the contents that hold it would need SHACL-SPARQL (a known limitation). Not here: the arrivals (Molecule_Time_R1), the opening (Molecule_Time_R2); that the material of the phase has a known origin is checked at the phase - the arrival time of a container content by Phase_Time_R1, an origin for every other phase by Phase_Time_R2 - and not again at every molecule, so a gap is reported once. An assignment (rule 7.16 of the crate rules): nothing is required, the shape never reports.</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R3_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R3_MainShape"/>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R1 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R1">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#AssignmentRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R1_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeTime"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Time R1 - Arrivals upstream</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Assigns to the time context of a molecule the arrival of every content and supply its phase's material may have come from. Nothing is required.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: since when have the chemicals been in the lab that this molecule can come from? Applies to: every Molecule. The counterpart of Phase_Time_R4 for the molecule: the same path and the same value shape, with one step in front - from the molecule up to its phase (dcterms:isPartOf). Assigns: every arrival on the way back - the occurrence at the Fixed library entry of a container content, which starts when the content was delivered or made in the lab and ends when it was used up or disposed of, and the permanent occurrence of a supply that is always there (the room air, the content of a supply line). The occurrence is linked; status, start and end are reached from it. Known limit, to be addressed later: the assignment works per phase, not per substance. A molecule is assigned every candidate of its phase, also contents that never held its substance - ammonia formed in a reactor is assigned the bottles of the reactants. Following only the transfers that move its substance (cpo:transfersChemicalEntity) and only the contents that hold it would need SHACL-SPARQL (a known limitation). Not here: the tasks that delivered or made the contents are the information counterpart (Molecule_Information_R6); that the material of the phase has a known origin is checked at the phase - the arrival time of a container content by Phase_Time_R1, an origin for every other phase by Phase_Time_R2 - and not again at every molecule, so a gap is reported once. An assignment (rule 7.16 of the crate rules): nothing is required, the shape never reports.</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R1_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R1_MainShape"/>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R4 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R4">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#AssignmentRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R4_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeTime"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Time R4 - Containers upstream on the same level</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Assigns to the time context of a molecule every chemical container of its own level its phase's material is in or may have come from. Nothing is required.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: from which containers of this experiment can this molecule come? Applies to: every Molecule. The counterpart of Phase_Time_R7 for the molecule: the same path and the same value shape, with one step in front - from the molecule up to its phase (dcterms:isPartOf). Assigns: the chemical containers (cpo:ChemicalContainer) of its own level that its phase's material is in or may have come from - backwards over the transfers of that level only, to the material and its container. The container is linked; its use before this level, its delivery and its opening are reached from it. Only the own level, unlike Molecule_Time_R1-R3: the containers that stood in this experiment or series, not their library entries. Known limit, to be addressed later: the assignment works per phase, not per substance. A molecule is assigned every candidate of its phase, also contents that never held its substance - ammonia formed in a reactor is assigned the bottles of the reactants. Following only the transfers that move its substance (cpo:transfersChemicalEntity) and only the contents that hold it would need SHACL-SPARQL (a known limitation). Not here: what is known about the origins along the whole way back - opening, expiry and arrival (Molecule_Time_R1-R3). An assignment (rule 7.16 of the crate rules): nothing is required, the shape never reports.</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R4_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Time_R4_MainShape"/>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R5 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R5">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#AssignmentRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R5_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeInformation"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Information R5 - Lots upstream</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Assigns to the information context of a molecule the material carrying the lot of every content its phase's material may have come from. Nothing is required.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: from which lots can this molecule come? Applies to: every Molecule. The counterpart of Phase_Information_R4 for the molecule: the same path and the same value shape, with one step in front - from the molecule up to its phase (dcterms:isPartOf). Assigns: the Fixed material that carries the lot (cpo:hasLotNumber) of every content its phase's material may have come from - backwards over the transfers and up the chain, any number of steps in any order, then to the material (dcterms:isPartOf) and its library entry. The material is linked, not the lot: a lot number is a text. Known limit, to be addressed later: the assignment works per phase, not per substance. A molecule is assigned every candidate of its phase, also contents that never held its substance - ammonia formed in a reactor is assigned the bottles of the reactants. Following only the transfers that move its substance (cpo:transfersChemicalEntity) and only the contents that hold it would need SHACL-SPARQL (a known limitation). Not here: the lot of a container content is required at the phase (Phase_Information_R2); that the material of the phase has a known origin is checked at the phase - the arrival time of a container content by Phase_Time_R1, an origin for every other phase by Phase_Time_R2 - and not again at every molecule, so a gap is reported once. An assignment (rule 7.16 of the crate rules): nothing is required, the shape never reports.</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R5_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R5_MainShape"/>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R6 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R6">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#AssignmentRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R6_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeInformation"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Information R6 - Origin tasks upstream</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Assigns to the information context of a molecule the task that delivered or produced every content its phase's material may have come from. Nothing is required.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: which tasks delivered or made what this molecule can come from? Applies to: every Molecule. The counterpart of Phase_Information_R5 for the molecule: the same path and the same value shape, with one step in front - from the molecule up to its phase (dcterms:isPartOf). Assigns: the Fixed task that delivered (receiving) or made (preparing in the lab) every content its phase's material may have come from - to the arrival of each content and from its start backwards over cpo:hasEndTime to the task that ends at that instant. The task is linked. Known limit, to be addressed later: the assignment works per phase, not per substance. A molecule is assigned every candidate of its phase, also contents that never held its substance - ammonia formed in a reactor is assigned the bottles of the reactants. Following only the transfers that move its substance (cpo:transfersChemicalEntity) and only the contents that hold it would need SHACL-SPARQL (a known limitation). Not here: the arrivals themselves are assigned by Molecule_Time_R1, the time counterpart; that the material of the phase has a known origin is checked at the phase - the arrival time of a container content by Phase_Time_R1, an origin for every other phase by Phase_Time_R2 - and not again at every molecule, so a gap is reported once. An assignment (rule 7.16 of the crate rules): nothing is required, the shape never reports.</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R6_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Information_R6_MainShape"/>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Structure_Substance_R1 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Structure_Substance_R1">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Structure_Substance_R1_MainShape"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Structure and Substance R1 - InChI</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every molecule has an InChI.</rdfs:comment>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeStructure"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeSubstance"/>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: what are the structure and the composition of this molecule, written so that every toolkit reads them the same way? Applies to: every Molecule, library entries included: the value stands once, at the Fixed entry in the chemical entity library, and every level reads it from there through hlo:specializationOf. Checks: the molecule reaches an InChI (cpo:hasInChI). Filed under Structure and Substance: the InChI encodes the connectivity, including stereochemistry, and the elemental composition. Reports: no InChI is found - also for a molecule that no database lists yet (accepted, a known limitation). Not here: the key databases look a molecule up by, the InChIKey (Molecule_Information_R4); that the value is a text starting with "InChI=" is checked in the data quality shapes (hlo:ChemicalEntity_Format_Shape, rule 2.8 of the crate rules). An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Structure_Substance_R1_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Structure_Substance_R1_MainShape"/>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R1 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R1">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R1_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeSpace"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Space R1 - Phase membership</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every molecule in use belongs to exactly one phase.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: in which phase is this molecule? Applies to: every Molecule in use. Not to Fixed molecules, the library entries (rule 7.7 of the crate rules); an entry of the chemical entity library describes a species in general and stands in no phase. Checks: the molecule is part of exactly one Phase (dcterms:isPartOf). A molecule record stands for one species in one phase; the same species in two phases is two records, each linked through hlo:specializationOf to the one library entry. Reports: a molecule in no phase, or in two at once - a modelling error, not a molecule in two places. Not here: that a phase has constituents is checked at the phase (Phase_Substance_R1). An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R1_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R1_MainShape"/>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R5 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R5">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R5_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeSpace"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Space R5 - Partial molar volume</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every molecule in use has an actual partial molar volume.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: how much volume does one mole of this species take up in its phase? Applies to: every Molecule in use. Not to Fixed molecules (rule 7.7 of the crate rules). Checks: the molecule has an Actual ProcessVariable for its partial molar volume (cpo:hasPartialMolarVolume) - the entity, not whether its value is known; evidence or a reference can be attached to it later. No QuantityKind is checked: QUDT has no PartialMolarVolume, and the property itself is unambiguous. Its values on the higher levels are collected along the chain of the Actual (Info). Reports: no such Actual. Not here: the volume of the phase as a whole (Phase_Space_R1). An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R5_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R5_MainShape"/>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R3 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R3">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R3_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeSpace"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Space R3 - Mass</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every molecule in use has an actual mass of its species in its phase.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: what mass of this species is in its phase? Applies to: every Molecule in use. Not to Fixed molecules (rule 7.7 of the crate rules). Checks: the molecule has an Actual ProcessVariable for the mass of its species in its phase (cpo:hasMass). The molecule record stands for one species in one phase, so its mass says how much of that species the phase holds - a statement about where the molecule is, filed under Space like the concentration. In a pure phase it is the very Actual of the phase's mass (Phase_Substance_R2). Its values on the higher levels are collected along the chain of the Actual (Info). Reports: no such Actual. Not here: the mass of the phase as a whole (Phase_Substance_R2), the molar mass that links mass and amount of substance (Molecule_Substance_R3). The phase is assigned the mass of each of its molecules (Phase_Substance_R4) but does not require it, so a missing one is reported once, here. An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R3_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R3_MainShape"/>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R4 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R4">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R4_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeSpace"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Space R4 - Amount of substance</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every molecule in use has an actual amount of substance of its species in its phase.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: what amount of substance of this species is in its phase? Applies to: every Molecule in use. Not to Fixed molecules (rule 7.7 of the crate rules). Checks: the molecule has an Actual ProcessVariable for the amount of substance of its species in its phase (cpo:hasAmountOfSubstance) - built like Molecule_Space_R3, but always an Actual of its own: a phase has no amount of substance to share, even when it is pure. Its values on the higher levels are collected along the chain of the Actual (Info). Reports: no such Actual. Not here: the mass (Molecule_Space_R3), the molar mass that links the two (Molecule_Substance_R3). The phase is assigned the amount of substance of each of its molecules (Phase_Substance_R4) but does not require it, so a missing one is reported once, here. An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R4_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R4_MainShape"/>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R2 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R2">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R2_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeSpace"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Space R2 - Concentration</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every molecule in use has an actual concentration.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: how much of this species is in its phase, as a share of the whole or as an amount? Applies to: every Molecule in use. Not to Fixed molecules (rule 7.7 of the crate rules). Checks: the molecule has an Actual ProcessVariable for its concentration (cpo:hasConcentration). Whether a value is a fraction or an absolute amount is said by its QuantityKind - one of those cpo:valueHasQuantityKind allows on cpo:hasConcentration - and checked on the values (shapes/QuantityKindShapes.ttl, rule 4.4), so one property is enough. The value stands at the molecule record of one phase, since a concentration means something only in one specific phase; filed under Space, since from the molecule's point of view it says how densely the molecule is distributed in the space it occupies. Its values on the higher levels are collected along the chain of the Actual (Info). Reports: no such Actual. Not here: the phase is assigned the concentrations of its molecules (Phase_Substance_R4) but does not require them, so a missing one is reported once, here. An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R2_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Space_R2_MainShape"/>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Structure_R2 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Structure_R2">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Structure_R2_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeStructure"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Structure R2 - SMILES</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every molecule has a SMILES string.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: what is the structure of this molecule in the notation most tools take as input? Applies to: every Molecule, library entries included: the value stands once, at the Fixed entry in the chemical entity library, and every level reads it from there through hlo:specializationOf. Checks: the molecule reaches a SMILES string (cpo:hasSMILES). SMILES is a structure notation, not an identifier: different toolkits write different strings for the same molecule; the unambiguous notation is the InChI (Molecule_Structure_Substance_R1). Reports: no SMILES is found. Not here: that the value is a text is checked in the data quality shapes (hlo:ChemicalEntity_Format_Shape, rule 2.8 of the crate rules). An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Structure_R2_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Structure_R2_MainShape"/>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Substance_R2 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Substance_R2">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Substance_R2_MainShape"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Substance R2 - Molecular formula</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every molecule has a molecular formula.</rdfs:comment>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeSubstance"/>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: which elements does this molecule consist of, and how many atoms of each? Applies to: every Molecule, library entries included: the value stands once, at the Fixed entry in the chemical entity library, and every level reads it from there through hlo:specializationOf. Checks: the molecule reaches a molecular formula (cpo:hasMolecularFormula). Together with the InChI it replaces modelling the atoms of a molecule as entities of their own: atoms are ChemicalEntities themselves, so a dcterms:hasPart link to them would make every molecule requirement apply to every atom. Filed under Substance: the formula states which atoms the molecule consists of - its constituents, as Phase_Substance_R1 and Device_Substance_R4 name those of a phase and of a body material; how the atoms are connected is structure (Molecule_Structure_Substance_R1, Molecule_Structure_R2). Reports: no formula is found. Not here: that the value is a text is checked in the data quality shapes (hlo:ChemicalEntity_Format_Shape, rule 2.8 of the crate rules). An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Substance_R2_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Substance_R2_MainShape"/>
    


    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Substance_R3 -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Substance_R3">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#ExistenceRequirement"/>
        <hasShape rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Substance_R3_MainShape"/>
        <requirementOf rdf:resource="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#MoleculeSubstance"/>
        <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Molecule Substance R3 - Molar mass</rdfs:label>
        <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Every molecule has a molar mass.</rdfs:comment>
        <schema:description xml:lang="en">Question: what is the mass of one mole of this substance? Applies to: every Molecule, library entries included: the value stands once, at the Fixed entry in the chemical entity library, and every level reads it from there through hlo:specializationOf. The molar mass is a cpo:Parameter with a reference to its source (PubChem); as a constant of the substance it is not repeated on every level. Checks: the molecule reaches a molar mass (cpo:hasMolarMass). Filed under Substance: it is the property of the substance that links the mass and the amount of substance of the species in its phase. Reports: no molar mass is found. Not here: that mass and that amount (Molecule_Space_R3, R4). An existence requirement (rule 7.16).</schema:description>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Substance_R3_MainShape -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="http://www.semanticweb.org/lf576/ontologies/2026/7/HigherLevelOntology#Molecule_Substance_R3_MainShape"/>



